<%# A lift with nothing but warmups recorded draws no chart, and a page that draws no
    chart is not worth half a megabyte of charting library, so the layout takes its cue
    from here rather than loading them for everyone. %>
<%- @charts = !@progress.empty? %>
<%# And this chart wants a real calendar, which costs another 215KB of date adapter (#434).
    A goal sits at a date in the future and the pace line runs to it, so an axis made of the
    days somebody happened to train cannot hold either. Its own flag rather than folding it
    into @charts, so the volume page -- whose weeks are evenly spaced categories and which
    parses no dates at all -- goes on not paying for it. %>
<%- @calendar = @charts %>
<div class="mx-auto max-w-md">
  <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-gray-900"><%= @exercise[:name] %></h1>
    <%- if @exercise.account_id == @account_id %>
      <a href="/exercises/<%= @exercise[:id] %>/edit" class="<%= button_style %> bg-lime-500 px-3 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">Edit</a>
    <%- else %>
      <span class="rounded-md bg-gray-100 px-3 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-gray-500">Built-in exercise</span>
    <%- end %>
  </div>

  <%- if provenance(@exercise) %>
    <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-gray-500"><%= provenance(@exercise) %></p>
  <%- end %>

  <%# What changing the per-side answer did to training already logged. #392.

      It reaches back and rewrites sets somebody has lifted, and moves the volume on those
      sessions by half, so it says so. A lifter who ticks "counted per side" and sees nothing
      happen cannot tell the fix from the bug they were hitting.

      amber because this is the colour this app warns in and nothing else (#214), and a line
      saying "12 logged sets were just rewritten" is the one thing on this page that has
      earned it. Read once and gone: it is taken out of the session by the route, so a reload
      does not re-announce an edit made ten minutes ago.

      role="status" rather than alert: it is the outcome of something the lifter just did and
      asked for, not an interruption. %>
  <%- if @notice %>
    <p role="status" class="mt-4 rounded-md border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-amber-800">
      <%= @notice %>
    </p>
  <%- end %>

  <%# Above the chart rather than below the sets, because the note is why the movement is
      in the program and the chart is how it is going -- one is read before the other.
      A note is free prose, typed either by the account or by the assistant it connected,
      and what was typed is rendered as the words that were typed -- the tag escapes it,
      so an h() here would escape it a second time and put the ampersands on the page.
      whitespace-pre-line keeps the line breaks somebody put between two cues while still
      wrapping a long line on a phone, which is the one thing pre would not do. %>
  <%- if @exercise.note %>
    <p class="mt-4 whitespace-pre-line text-sm text-gray-700"><%= @exercise.note %></p>
  <%- end %>

  <%# What the app is assuming about this movement, and what the assumption costs. #439.

      Here rather than on the session row, and the difference is who is being interrupted. The
      row is read one-handed between sets and must carry what this set is; this is a setup
      question, read once, and the page it is answered on is one tap away.

      Not amber. Nothing has gone wrong and nothing has been rewritten -- the default is the
      cautious one and every weight it has prescribed is loadable. What is worth saying is that
      the cautious answer has a ceiling, and that a movement sitting on that ceiling looks
      exactly like a movement that has stopped progressing.

      Both ranges, because the gap between them is the whole argument and neither number means
      much alone. %>
  <%- if (assumed = unanswered_dumbbells(@exercise)) %>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-md border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
      Nothing has said how many dumbbells this movement is done with, so the app assumes
      <strong>two</strong> &mdash; the cautious answer, since anything a pair can load a single
      can load too. On your rack a pair reaches <strong><%= assumed[:pair] %></strong> and one
      reaches <strong><%= assumed[:one] %></strong>, so nothing above
      <strong><%= assumed[:ceiling] %> lb</strong> can be prescribed here until you say.
      <%# The link only where it would work. A library movement belongs to everybody, so the
          edit route refuses it -- and a page that says "say how many" to somebody who cannot
          is worse than one that reports the ceiling and stops. The fact is true either way,
          which is why the sentence above is not also gated. %>
      <%- if @exercise.account_id == @account_id %>
        <a href="/exercises/<%= @exercise[:id] %>/edit" class="font-semibold text-sky-800 underline">Say how many</a>
        if that is not right.
      <%- end %>
    </p>
  <%- end %>

  <%# The number a percentage of this movement resolves against, and a box to state it.
      #264.

      Above the history rather than below it, because it is a setting and the rest of this
      page is a record -- and because the case it exists for is the one where there is no
      history to sit under. Coming off a layoff, or on a movement never logged, everything
      below here is empty and this is the only thing on the page worth reading.

      On a library movement as well as an account's own, unlike the Edit button at the top.
      That is the whole reason #264 is a table keyed on (account, movement) rather than a
      column on the movement: a shared Back Squat carries a different max for every account
      that lifts it, so there is nothing here for the library rule to protect.

      Three states, and the page says which one it is in rather than showing a number and
      leaving its provenance to be guessed. A stated max is a fact about the lifter. A
      derived one is an inference the app is making, and should be seen to be making, since
      it is the one that goes stale over a layoff -- which is the case this feature is for.
      Neither is worth saying plainly too: it is the state in which a percentage lift
      refuses to generate, and the refusal names this page. %>
  <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Training max</h2>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
    <%- if @training_max&.stated? %>
      Percentages of this movement are worked out from <strong><%= weight_label(@training_max.pounds) %> lb</strong>,
      <%= @training_max.stated_phrase %>.
      Clear the box to go back to estimating it from your lifting.
    <%- elsif @training_max %>
      Percentages of this movement are worked out from <strong><%= weight_label(@training_max.pounds) %> lb</strong>,
      an estimated one-rep max<%= @training_max.since('from a set on') %>
      &mdash; your best ever, with no cutoff, so it does not fade with time off. Set one below
      to override it, which is worth doing coming back from a layoff, when that estimate reads
      work you did before the break.
    <%- elsif @estimate %>
      <%# The state this page most recently got wrong, in its third telling.
          It first said "nothing has been lifted here that the chart can read" to somebody with
          two completed sets on the movement -- untrue, and followed by advice they had already
          taken. That was corrected to say the sets were there and the estimate could not read
          them, which was true at the time.
          It is no longer true. The chart reads to ten reps now, so those sets do produce a
          number; what they do not produce is a number the app will hang a block off without
          being asked, because everything generated as a percentage of it inherits an error
          made nine reps from a single and compounds it week to week.
          So the number is shown. Withholding it was the dead end, and showing it while
          pretending it is a training max would be the opposite mistake. %>
      <%= logged_working_sets %> completed working
      <%= logged_working_sets == 1 ? 'set is' : 'sets are' %> logged here, and they estimate a
      one-rep max of about <strong><%= weight_label(@estimate[:pounds]) %> lb</strong>
      &mdash; read from a set as hard as <%= @estimate[:from_reps] %> reps taken to two in
      reserve. That is far enough from a single that the app will not make it the number your
      percentages come off, because everything written against it would inherit the guess. Set
      a max below to use one, or log a heavier set of five or fewer.
    <%- elsif logged_working_sets.positive? %>
      <%# Four states, not three. This branch used to share the one below and said "nothing has
          been lifted here that the chart can read" -- which a lifter reads as "nothing has been
          lifted here", and which was followed by the advice "log a completed set" to somebody
          who had logged two.

          It was reported against a Bulgarian Split Squat carrying two completed working sets of
          44x8 at RPE 7. The estimate restates a rated set as the RPE-8 set of equal difficulty
          -- eight reps at 7 is nine at 8 -- and SetScheme's table stops at five, so neither set
          could be read. The app was right that it had no number and wrong about why, and the
          only thing it told the lifter to do was the thing they had already done twice.

          So this says what is actually true: the sets are there, they are outside what the
          estimate covers, and the way out is the box directly below. The count is in it
          deliberately -- an app claiming nothing was logged while the set list sits further
          down the same page is an app the lifter stops believing. %>
      <%= logged_working_sets %> completed working
      <%= logged_working_sets == 1 ? 'set is' : 'sets are' %> logged here, but the estimate
      cannot read <%= logged_working_sets == 1 ? 'it' : 'them' %>: it works from sets of about
      five reps or fewer taken close to your limit, and treats higher-rep work as too far from a
      single to guess from. So a lift written as a percentage of this movement cannot be
      generated yet. Set a max below &mdash; that is the fix for a movement you train in eights
      &mdash; or log a heavier set of five or fewer.
    <%- elsif unfinished_sets.positive? %>
      <%# The state the original report was actually about, and the one the first two attempts
          at this sentence both missed.

          Exercise 1 carries thirteen squat sets logged between 2023 and 2025, none of them
          ticked off. They are drawn in the table further down this very page, and the
          paragraph above them said "nothing has been lifted here" -- then, after the first
          fix, "nothing has been logged here yet", which is the same untruth in new words.

          The app is not wrong to ignore them. A set written and never marked done is, by this
          app's model, training that was planned rather than performed, and an estimate must
          not come from a set nobody has said happened. What it is wrong about is describing
          that as an absence, when the sets are on the screen.

          So this names the number, names the reason, and gives the action that actually
          works -- ticking a set off, which is a thing the set editor does. %>
      <%= unfinished_sets %> <%= unfinished_sets == 1 ? 'set is' : 'sets are' %> logged here,
      <%# Both halves say "not", which the first draft of the singular did not: it read "it has
          been marked as done", the exact opposite of the state it describes, while the plural
          beside it was right. A spec caught it. Negation carried by a word that only appears in
          one branch of a ternary is worth writing out on both sides. %>
      but <%= unfinished_sets == 1 ? 'it has not' : 'none of them has' %> been marked as done. The
      app reads an unticked set as training that was written down rather than performed, so
      nothing here can be estimated from yet.
      Mark <%= unfinished_sets == 1 ? 'it' : 'them' %> done from the set page, or set a max
      below.
    <%- else %>
      Nothing has been logged here yet, so a lift written as a percentage of this movement
      cannot be generated yet. Set a max below, or log a completed set.
    <%- end %>
  </p>

  <%# What scale the box is on, said plainly, because the app cannot know which convention
      you train on and must not guess.

      A training max and a one-rep max are different numbers -- 5/3/1 puts the training max at
      about 90% of a tested single, and every percentage it writes is of that -- while Sheiko
      and the percentage programming it comes out of are written against a competition max
      directly. The same "80%" means two loads about ten percent apart depending which was
      meant, and nothing in a number says which it is.

      So this says what the number is used for and gives the two conventions, rather than
      applying a coefficient. Discounting a stated max on the lifter's behalf would be the app
      picking 0.85 or 0.90 for somebody whose programme may have already picked one -- and it
      would silently move the loads of everybody who has been typing an undiscounted number. %>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
    This is the number percentages are taken of. A 5/3/1-style block wants roughly 90% of a
    one-rep max, which you can say as two numbers &mdash; your max, and the percentage of it
    you train off. If you program off a competition max, put it in and leave the percentage
    blank.
  </p>

  <%# min-h-11 is 44px and text-base is 16px, which is the threshold below which iOS Safari
      zooms the page when a field takes focus and leaves it zoomed. Both match the block
      editor's row; see views/programs/_lift.erb. step="any" because this is a number the
      lifter knows rather than one the rack has to build -- what a bar can be loaded to is a
      different question, asked at generation. %>
  <form method="post" action="/exercises/<%= @exercise[:id] %>/training-max" class="mt-3 flex items-end gap-2">
    <%== csrf_tag("/exercises/#{@exercise[:id]}/training-max") %>
    <label class="text-xs text-gray-500" for="training-max">
      Pounds
      <input type="number" name="pounds" id="training-max" min="0" max="2000" step="any" autocomplete="off"
             value="<%= weight_label(@training_max.stated_pounds) if @training_max&.stated? %>" placeholder="lb"
             class="<%= field_style %> mt-1 block min-h-11 w-28 px-2 text-base text-gray-900">
    </label>
    <%# The second half of the prescription, and the reason both boxes exist rather than one.
        A 5/3/1 lifter says 500 and 90 and the page shows "500 lb x 90%" back; a lifter
        programming off a competition max says 500 and leaves this alone. Typing the
        discounted number straight into the box above still works and always did -- 100 is
        the default and multiplies out to itself -- so this adds a way to say it rather than
        a way it must be said.

        Blank means the whole number, which is what TrainingMax.fraction decides for every
        write path: a lifter who clears this box is saying "off the number I typed", not
        refusing to answer. %>
    <label class="text-xs text-gray-500" for="train-at">
      Train at
      <input type="number" name="train_at_percent" id="train-at" min="50" max="100" step="1" autocomplete="off"
             value="<%= @training_max.train_at_percent if @training_max&.discounted? %>" placeholder="%"
             class="<%= field_style %> mt-1 block min-h-11 w-20 px-2 text-base text-gray-900">
    </label>
    <button type="submit" class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 bg-lime-500 px-3 text-sm font-semibold
                                  text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">Save</button>
  </form>

  <%# Sets that count their reps the other way from the movement itself. #392.

      Amber, which is the colour this app warns in and nothing else (#214), and this has
      earned it: every set here is contributing half the work it did to every volume figure
      on the site, silently, and has been since it was written.

      Not a button. The repair that runs when a movement's answer *changes* can tell a stale
      set from a deliberate one, because it has the old answer to compare against. A standing
      one has no such discriminator, and somebody who really did both legs at once on a
      machine is entitled to have said so. So this says what is true and leaves the correction
      a decision -- the set editor and update_set both make it, one set at a time. %>
  <%- if (adrift = per_side_disagreement(@exercise)).positive? %>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-md border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-amber-800">
      <strong><%= adrift %> completed <%= adrift == 1 ? 'set counts' : 'sets count' %></strong>
      <%= @exercise.default_is_per_side ? 'both sides together' : 'per side' %>, where this
      movement says <%= @exercise.default_is_per_side ? 'per side' : 'both sides together' %>.
      Volume reads <%= @exercise.default_is_per_side ? 'half' : 'twice' %> the work for
      <%= adrift == 1 ? 'it' : 'them' %> until <%= adrift == 1 ? 'it is' : 'they are' %>
      corrected, which is done a set at a time from the set page.
    </p>
  <%- end %>

  <%# How long you rest on this movement. #456.
      Here rather than only on the edit form, and for the training max's reason: the edit form
      is owner-only and has to be, because a name or a note written to a library row is one
      account's answer on everybody's page. A rest is not that kind of fact -- 039 keys it on
      (account, movement) the way 020 keys the max -- so it belongs on the page every movement
      has rather than on the one only your own movements have.

      That is the whole of the fix rather than a convenience. Nine of the movements in this
      block are library rows, Back Squat and Bench Press among them, and while this field lived
      on the edit form the bell could be switched on for accessories and never for the lifts
      where three minutes actually matters. %>
  <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Rest between sets</h2>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
    <%- if @rest %>
      The session screen counts <strong><%= Tectonic::Timing.named(@rest) %></strong> down after
      you tick a set of this off, and rings when it is up. Clear the box for no bell.
    <%- else %>
      Nothing set, so no bell rings after a set of this. Name a length and the session screen
      counts it down from the moment you tick a set off &mdash; the app will not pick one for
      you, because a countdown that rings unasked is it deciding how long you rest.
    <%- end %>
  </p>

  <form method="post" action="/exercises/<%= @exercise[:id] %>/rest" class="mt-3 flex items-end gap-2">
    <%== csrf_tag("/exercises/#{@exercise[:id]}/rest") %>
    <label class="text-xs text-gray-500" for="rest-seconds">
      Seconds
      <input type="number" name="seconds" id="rest-seconds" min="5" max="1800" step="5" autocomplete="off"
             value="<%= @rest %>" placeholder="s"
             class="<%= field_style %> mt-1 block min-h-11 w-28 px-2 text-base text-gray-900">
    </label>
    <button type="submit" class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 bg-lime-500 px-3 text-sm font-semibold
                                  text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">Save</button>
  </form>

  <%# What you are aiming at, and when by. #308.

      One paragraph below the training max, because the goal is a target for that number and
      the two are read together: "worked out from 450 lb" and "aiming at 500 by March 14" is
      the whole of "am I on pace" on one movement, and the block-by-block openers are the
      rest of it.

      How far there is to go is shown as a subtraction and nothing more. Nothing here divides
      pounds by weeks to call a lifter behind: pace on a barbell is not linear, a peaking
      block moves a max in a way a hypertrophy block deliberately does not, and an app that
      did the arithmetic would call a correctly-run offseason a failure every time. The app
      reports the gap and the days; the judgement is somebody else's, which is #263. %>
  <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Goal</h2>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
    <%- if @goal %>
      Aiming at <strong><%= weight_label(@goal.pounds) %> lb</strong><%= @goal.deadline_phrase %>.
      <%= @goal.gap_phrase(@training_max&.pounds) %>
      <%= @goal.days_phrase %>
      Clear the box to drop it.
    <%- else %>
      Nothing set. A number and a date here is what makes &ldquo;am I on pace&rdquo; a question
      with an answer &mdash; without one the app can show what you have been training against
      and cannot say whether it is enough.
    <%- end %>
  </p>

  <form method="post" action="/exercises/<%= @exercise[:id] %>/goal" class="mt-3 flex items-end gap-2">
    <%== csrf_tag("/exercises/#{@exercise[:id]}/goal") %>
    <label class="text-xs text-gray-500" for="goal-pounds">
      Pounds
      <input type="number" name="pounds" id="goal-pounds" min="0" max="2000" step="any" autocomplete="off"
             value="<%= weight_label(@goal.pounds) if @goal %>" placeholder="lb"
             class="<%= field_style %> mt-1 block min-h-11 w-28 px-2 text-base text-gray-900">
    </label>
    <%# Optional, because a target with no deadline is still a target and insisting on one
        would be the app requiring a plan the lifter has not made. %>
    <label class="text-xs text-gray-500" for="goal-by">
      By
      <input type="date" name="by_date" id="goal-by" autocomplete="off"
             value="<%= @goal&.by_date %>"
             class="<%= field_style %> mt-1 block min-h-11 w-40 px-2 text-base text-gray-900">
    </label>
    <button type="submit" class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 bg-lime-500 px-3 text-sm font-semibold
                                  text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">Save</button>
  </form>

  <%- if @sets.empty? %>
    <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-gray-500">No sets logged yet.</p>
  <%- else %>
    <%- if @charts %>
      <div class="mt-6">
        <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Progress</h2>
        <%# The chart #434 asks for, and it replaces "heaviest weight each day" rather than
            sitting beside it. That chart's line is this chart's first series, so keeping both
            would be the same training drawn twice a few inches apart -- and a reader would
            have to work out that the two agreed.

            **It gives up `discrete: true`, which the old chart argued for and was right to.**
            That argument was about a different question: whether the weight is going up, for
            which evenly-spaced sessions read better and a five-month layoff collapsing to one
            gap costs nothing. This chart's question is whether the weight is going up *fast
            enough*, and that one is about elapsed time -- so a layoff has to look like a
            layoff, and the goal has to sit at its own date rather than one column to the
            right of the last session.

            min: nil is kept, and for the reason the old note gives: nobody lifts zero, and an
            axis starting there flattens a season of progress into the top inch. %>
        <div aria-hidden="true">
          <%== line_chart(progress_series, ytitle: 'Weight', curve: false, min: nil, colors: Tectonic::CHART_COLORS) %>
        </div>
        <%# Unlike the old chart, this one carries things the table below does not: what each
            block opened at, the goal, and the pace line. So it gets a table of its own (#337)
            rather than an aria-hidden canvas and a pointer at the set list. %>
        <%== render('_chart_dates_table',
                    locals: { series: @progress, caption: "#{@exercise[:name]} progress against the goal" }) %>
        <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-gray-500">
          Heaviest completed working set each session. Every set is listed below, newest first.
        </p>
      </div>
    <%- end %>

    <table class="mt-6 min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-300">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col" class="py-3.5 pr-3 text-left text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Date</th>
          <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-3.5 text-left text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Weight</th>
          <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-3.5 text-left text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Reps</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200">
        <%- @sets.each do |set| %>
          <tr>
            <%# The day the set was lifted, not the day a programme asked for it. #572 calls
                this the worst of the seven, and it is right to: this table is a lifter's own
                record of what they lifted and when, and it is the one people read to answer
                "when did I last squat this". Dating it by plan answers a question nobody asked.

                The route eager-loads these workouts `with_performed_on`, so the stamp is on
                the row and Workout#performed_or_planned_on never falls back to a query here --
                which in a table of a year of sets would be a year of queries. %>
            <td class="whitespace-nowrap py-4 pr-3 text-sm text-gray-500">
              <%= set.workout.performed_or_planned_on.strftime('%b %d, %Y') %>
            </td>
            <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-3 py-4 text-sm text-gray-500"><%= weight_label(set[:weight]) %></td>
            <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-3 py-4 text-sm text-gray-500"><%= set[:reps] %></td>
          </tr>
        <%- end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  <%- end %>
</div>
